   130	
   131	The proof logs `connection-proof.out`, `loop-proof.out`, and
   132	`target-proof.out` each contain `#Top`.
   133	
   134	Negative checks:
   135	
   136	```text
   137	spec-vacuity.k:                exit 1, WarnStuckClaimState
   138	mutation-spec.k:               exit 1, WarnStuckClaimState
   139	connection-mutation-spec.k:    exit 1, WarnStuckClaimState
   140	```
   141	
   142	The first changes the result to `boredoms(IS) +Int 1`; the residual contains
   143	the rejected equality `finishScan(...) +Int 1 == finishScan(...)`. The second
   144	replaces the loop body with an empty body. The third changes `strip` to
   145	`upper`; on a space character fixed execution reaches `false`, not the claimed
   146	`true`.
   147	
   148	The toolchain was K `v7.1.293`.
   149	
   150	## Gate results
   151	
   152	- Gate A — PASS. Exact program KORE identity holds. Every operational bridge
   153	  has a bridge-free universal connection theorem over its complete match
   154	  domain. Equations are exhaustive, disjoint where guarded, and structurally
   155	  descending. Empty input is a realizable witness. Result, body, and
   156	  connection mutations are all rejected.
   157	- Gate B — PASS. The theorem covers every finite MPY `IntSeq`, not bounded
   158	  lengths. Its scanner-state meaning is exactly the prompt property under the
   159	  supplied delimiter and whitespace model. `solution.py` uses Python
   160	  `strip()`, so the implementation itself follows Python whitespace behavior.
   161	- Gate C — PASS. Commands, outputs, negative probes, identity evidence,
   162	  differential evidence, and the fixed-model boundary are reproducible and
   163	  explicitly separated below.
   164	
   165	## Trust boundary
   166	
   167	The theorem is conditional on the supplied reference semantics, K frontend,
   168	Haskell backend, and their built-in integer, Boolean, string, map, and
   169	reachability machinery. No program-defined operation is left opaque, and no
   170	unproved result-bearing oracle is introduced.
   171	
   172	The supplied MPY `isWSC` recognizes only codes 32, 9, 10, and 13, whereas
   173	CPython `str.strip()` recognizes additional whitespace. This is witnessed by
   174	`"I\vwork"`:
   175	
   176	```text
   177	CPython solution.py result: 1
   178	MPY model-boundary.mpy result: 0, krun exit 0
   179	```
   180	
   181	Thus the K theorem is about all values of the supplied model, while equivalence
   182	to CPython on additional Unicode/ASCII whitespace remains conditional on this
   183	documented model boundary. No input length or MPY-representable code is removed
   184	from the formal theorem.
   185	
   186	## Empirical evidence
   187	
   188	`differential.py` compares the state-machine implementation with an independent
   189	sentence-split/word-split oracle. It checks the prompt examples, all strings of
   190	length 0 through 5 over an eight-character boundary alphabet, and 5,000
   191	deterministic random strings including non-ASCII and additional whitespace:
   192	
   193	```text
   194	differential cases=42456 mismatches=0
   195	```
   196	
   197	`smoke.py` also executes under the required LLVM definition and covers empty or
   198	ordinary sentences, multiple delimiter kinds, leading modeled whitespace,
   199	standalone `I`, `Idea`, and a later non-leading `I`.
   200	
   201	These finite checks support intent and model adequacy; the universal result is
   202	established by the K claims, not by testing.
   203	
   204	## Excluded behavior
   205	
   206	Non-string arguments are outside the prompt’s stated input type. Total
   207	correctness/termination and fidelity outside the supplied MPY model are not
   208	claimed. The K theorem has no finite-length, fixed-example, or bounded-unrolling
   209	restriction.

